65
00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:45.000
you might get a promotion in various ways. In most schools, salaries are basically set in lockstep to experience, with no ability for the principles to have discretion to give teachers a bonus if they're doing extremely well or try to give a retention package to a teacher who was really attractive offer from a private school or from a totally different employer. So I think creating a little bit more of that flexibility to retain and recruit top talent in teaching, just like we do in the rest of the economy in the United States,

66
00:34:45.000 --> 00:35:13.000
could be incredibly valuable. But I think, you know, more broadly, what I see as the broader problem is teaching is often not viewed as the first profession you'd choose to pursue if you're a really talented kid in the United States. And I think that's really not a good state of affairs. Countries that have excellent educational systems that people point to Finland, for example, you often hear that kids' aspiration if they're doing really well in school is to become a teacher.

67
00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:47.000
